Hobbes makes protection a central purpose of common political power. Follow his argument and examine what happens to obedience when protection fails.

C0092 | atlas-focused-sessions-v6/skip bridge/consent-rule/onAlreadyComplete.text
Hobbes and Locke offer different limits on authority, protection and resistance.

C0093 | atlas-focused-sessions-v6/story context heading/consent-rule/entryCue.contextHeading
England, 1651–1690

C0094 | atlas-focused-sessions-v6/objective/consent-rule-session-2/objectives
When may people resist a government?

C0095 | atlas-focused-sessions-v6/entry fallback/consent-rule-session-2/motivation; atlas-focused-sessions-v6/story body/consent-rule-session-2/entryCue.text
It is evening. The worktable is cleared for a new session. Compare Locke's consent and right of resistance with Hobbes's account. Their limits on authority are not identical.

C0096 | atlas-focused-sessions-v6/objective/longitude/objectives
Can the Moon help locate a ship?
Can a clock keep reference time at sea?

C0097 | atlas-focused-sessions-v6/entry fallback/longitude/motivation; atlas-focused-sessions-v6/story body/longitude/entryCue.text
These records cover c. 1714–1770 CE. Compare using a reference clock with using observations of the Moon to find longitude. Read the sea-trial record: a clock's workshop performance is not enough to show that it works on a voyage.

C0098 | atlas-focused-sessions-v6/objective/longitude-session-2/objectives
What makes an observatory useful?
What keeps a long voyage going?

C0099 | atlas-focused-sessions-v6/entry fallback/longitude-session-2/motivation; atlas-focused-sessions-v6/story body/longitude-session-2/entryCue.text
It is evening. The worktable is cleared for a new session. Compare astronomical work at Jaipur with the methods used for a voyage. Add longitude to the route and provide food for the journey.

C0100 | atlas-focused-sessions-v6/objective/public-knowledge/objectives
How are different kinds of knowledge connected?
Who can challenge a public argument?

C0101 | atlas-focused-sessions-v6/entry fallback/public-knowledge/motivation; atlas-focused-sessions-v6/story body/public-knowledge/entryCue.text
Craft illustrations and linked articles make practical knowledge more widely available. Consider who can read and challenge these public arguments.

C0102 | atlas-focused-sessions-v6/story context heading/public-knowledge/entryCue.contextHeading
Enlightenment debates, c. 1750–1792 CE

C0103 | atlas-focused-sessions-v6/objective/public-knowledge-session-2/objectives
What makes experience possible?
Is having enough the same as having status?

C0104 | atlas-focused-sessions-v6/entry fallback/public-knowledge-session-2/motivation; atlas-focused-sessions-v6/story body/public-knowledge-session-2/entryCue.text
It is evening. The worktable is cleared for a new session. Hume questions why a past pattern should hold in the next case, and Kant offers a response about experience. A separate comparison asks whether status and luxury make a good life.

C0105 | atlas-focused-sessions-v6/objective/public-knowledge-session-3/objectives
Do universal rights include women?
Can universal liberty coexist with slavery?

C0106 | atlas-focused-sessions-v6/entry fallback/public-knowledge-session-3/motivation; atlas-focused-sessions-v6/story body/public-knowledge-session-3/entryCue.text
The next morning, we return to a cleared worktable. Compare a claim of universal rights with women's exclusion and enslaved people's testimony. Test whom the promise includes in practice.

C0107 | atlas-focused-sessions-v6/objective/exchange/objectives
Does more wealth make a better life?

C0108 | atlas-focused-sessions-v6/entry fallback/exchange/motivation; atlas-focused-sessions-v6/story body/exchange/entryCue.text
This comparison covers c. 1705–1800 CE. These economic accounts distinguish greater trade and wealth from living well. Compare Mandeville's claim about private motives with the older ideal of having enough.

C0109 | atlas-focused-sessions-v6/objective/exchange-session-2/objectives
What does work do to the worker?

C0110 | atlas-focused-sessions-v6/entry fallback/exchange-session-2/motivation; atlas-focused-sessions-v6/story body/exchange-session-2/entryCue.text
It is evening. The worktable is cleared for a new session. Compare the output gained by specialized tasks with their effects on workers. Producing more does not answer every question about a person's life.

C0111 | atlas-focused-sessions-v6/objective/exchange-session-3/objectives
Who should bear a shortage?
How free is this trade?

C0112 | atlas-focused-sessions-v6/entry fallback/exchange-session-3/motivation; atlas-focused-sessions-v6/story body/exchange-session-3/entryCue.text
The next morning, we return to a cleared worktable. Compare long voyages and traded goods with the forced labour used to produce them. When resources are scarce, who bears the cost?

C0113 | atlas-focused-sessions-v6/objective/freedom-empire/objectives
How does freedom become law?

