These records cover c. 1820–1895. Experiments link electricity and magnetism. Compare those results with a field account that describes influence through space and connects it with light.

C0139 | atlas-focused-sessions-v6/objective/fields-images-session-2/objectives
How can distant clocks agree?

C0140 | atlas-focused-sessions-v6/entry fallback/fields-images-session-2/motivation; atlas-focused-sessions-v6/story body/fields-images-session-2/entryCue.text
It is evening. The worktable is cleared for a new session. Electrical signals carry a reference time to distant clocks. Railway timetables show why separate local noons are not enough for coordination.

C0141 | atlas-focused-sessions-v6/objective/fields-images-session-3/objectives
What does a photograph leave out?
What changes when an image has many copies?

C0142 | atlas-focused-sessions-v6/entry fallback/fields-images-session-3/motivation; atlas-focused-sessions-v6/story body/fields-images-session-3/entryCue.text
The next morning, we return to a cleared worktable. A light-sensitive surface records an image. Compare the chosen frame with the changes made possible by producing many copies.

C0143 | atlas-focused-sessions-v6/objective/experience/objectives
How does one thought lead to another?
Does a clock describe all of time?

